  What is Meridian's standing gros         yes         NO        ok     WRONG

  where the failures actually are:

    nothing — it passed       5 of 14
    retrieval                 4 of 14
    the span alone            3 of 14
    the surrounding context   2 of 14

Four questions, asked in that order, and the first 'no' is the diagnosis.

  is it in the corpus?      if not, no amount of prompt work helps and the
                            fix is an ingestion ticket
  did retrieval fetch it?   if not, generation is innocent — it answered
                            correctly about what it was shown
  given the cited span,     if not, the span is evidence rather than context.
  can it answer?            One row of a table is exactly where the fact is
                            and not enough to read it without the headers
  given the real context,   if this is the only 'no', something in the other
  can it answer?            passages is distracting it — the most interesting
                            failure of the four, and the one people never
                            look for

Three of those four checks need no model at all, and the fourth is one call.
The expensive habit is arguing about whether the model is bad at contracts
before establishing whether the contract was in the context.

Note the rows where the cited span fails and the full context succeeds. Those
are not generation bugs and they are not retrieval bugs — they are a golden
set recording the narrowest evidence for an answer rather than enough of the
document to act on it. Chapter 21's spans were built to verify cases, and
this is where that choice shows through.
